-- title:
--   Inertia sends prime-to-p torsion into the toric subgroup
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and $M\ge 1$ with $p\mid M$ and $p^2\nmid M$, and a subgroup $H\le(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit that reduces to $1$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$; assume the Laurent series `jqModC` over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, and let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a Deligne–Rapoport-type integral model datum `XHDRModelAtP` for level $\Gamma_H(M)$ at $p$, which in particular supplies a curve model `Meta` of the function field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot F(\Gamma_H(M))$ together with an isomorphism `eeta` onto the geometric fibre. Further data: an automorphism $\theta_0$ of that function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ acting on the level-$M/p$ subfield by $q\mapsto q^p$ (hypothesis `hθ₀`, via `qExpand`), and the pin `hwgen₀` saying that the Atkin–Lehner morphism $\mathfrak{X}.w$ moves $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of `Meta.C` exactly as `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut θ₀` moves the associated places; a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit and algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$; level data $\Lambda$ and a Néron object $O$ over it for $J_H(M)$. The remaining hypotheses, summarised here, assert: the designation $(O.G,O.g,\text{unit section})$ represents the relative Picard subfunctor of $\mathfrak{X}$ rigidified along the cusp $\varepsilon_\infty$ and cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero, both over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ (`hD`) and after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ (`hDQ`, with separatedness `hsep` and the Poincaré comparison `hpoinc`); a morphism `ajQ` over the generic fibre, together with `hajQε` and `hajQ`, identifying it as the Abel–Jacobi map $x\mapsto[x-\varepsilon_\infty]$ on points with values in any field; base-change comparison `kQ` with `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`; a morphism `ajbar` from `Meta.C` to $O.G$ obtained from these (`hajbar`, `hajbar_over`), a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\bar\varepsilon$ lying over the cusp with $\bar\varepsilon\circ$`ajbar` the unit section (`hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`); additivity of `O.pts` for the relative group law attached to `hD` (`hpts_law`); and `hAJ`, that for each point $x$ and each cusp point $s$ the class of the degree-zero divisor $[\,x\,]-[\,s\,]$ is carried by `O.pts` to $x$ followed by `ajbar`. The conclusion: for every $m$ coprime to $p$, every $\sigma$ in the image in $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $A$, and every $m$-torsion class $x$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field, the difference $\sigma\cdot x-x$ lies in `O.toricPts m`, the subgroup generated by the range of `O.toricPoint m`.
--
--   This is the Grothendieck orthogonality statement at a prime of multiplicative reduction: inertia at $p$ acts trivially on $J_H(M)$ modulo the toric part, so that for $m$ prime to $p$ it carries $m$-torsion into the toric subgroup of the Néron model. It feeds the level-lowering analysis of the ramification of the mod-$\ell$ representation at $p$ and is used by [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_forall_mem_toricPts_exists_smul_sub_eq_of_coprime_of_abelJacobiPin_of_atkinLehner`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_forall_mem_toricPts_exists_smul_sub_eq_of_coprime_of_abelJacobiPin_of_atkinLehner).
